// @check
// @hint redundant type annotation: `xOf` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `sumXY` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `greet` is inferred as `string`
// @error sets `y`, `z`, which {x: i32} does not declare
// @error sets `z`, which {x: i32, y: i32} does not declare
// @error sets `id`, which {name: string} does not declare
// CONTRACT (must-error): a VALUE carrying MORE fields than the declared type it flows
// into is REJECTED. `assignable` still accepts the pair — structural width subtyping is a
// sound TYPE rule, and the callee only ever reads its own declared fields — but no VL
// value can reach that slot, so the rule has no value-level lowering and the checker says
// so instead of letting the program build.
//
// THIS FILE USED TO PIN THE OPPOSITE, under the name `struct-width-subtyping-sound.vl`,
// and its own text said the extra field "is silently ignored at the call boundary". That
// is true only while every extra field is a constant, which is the condition the file
// happened to hold itself to: an extra field whose initializer is a CALL had that call
// dropped along with the field, so a program that checked clean, built a valid module and
// printed the right number never ran a call the author wrote. The two halves of the rule
// landed separately —
//   · a source whose shape is PINNED (a parameter, a call result, a field read, an
//     annotated binding) emits invalid wasm and is rejected by the object-shape floor;
//     `objects/object-width-value-source-forms.vl` pins those,
//   · a source that is an object LITERAL is built at the destination's shape, which is
//     exactly what discards the excess field and its initializer;
//     `objects/object-literal-excess-field-reject.vl` pins those, and
//     `objects/object-literal-declared-field-initializer-runs.vl` pins the evaluation
//     that must still happen one field name away.
// All three call sites below are literals, so all three take the literal verdict.
//
// The way out is the one the diagnostic names: give the value exactly the destination's
// fields. `objects/object-literal-built-at-target-shape.vl` is the accepted neighbour —
// a literal whose field NAMES match, in any order and with a widening field type.
function xOf(p: { x: i32 }): i32 { return p.x }
function sumXY(p: { x: i32, y: i32 }): i32 { return p.x + p.y }

// Two extra fields — only `x` is declared.
print(xOf({ x: 3, y: 4, z: 5 }))

// One extra field over a two-field signature.
print(sumXY({ x: 3, y: 4, z: 5 }))

// Mixed types — the extra field is a string-keyed scalar, same verdict.
function greet(p: { name: string }): string { return p.name }
print(greet({ name: "hello", id: 42 }))
